<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry IV of England</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1367">1367</a>–<a href="/years/1413">1413</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Henry Bolingbroke · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-plantagenet">House of Plantagenet</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, Henry Bolingbroke, deposed his cousin Richard II in 1399 and founded the Lancastrian dynasty. His reign was spent putting down rebellions by the Percys and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-glyndwr">Owain Glyndŵr</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>. In 1405 he granted the Isle of Man to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-stanley-died-1414">Sir John Stanley</a>, whose family ruled it as Kings and Lords of Mann for centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-england.jpg" alt="Henry IV of England" width="320" height="382" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The National Archives UK · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Illumination_of_Henry_IV_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Henry was born at Bolingbroke Castle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lincolnshire">Lincolnshire</a> in about April 1367, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-gaunt">John of Gaunt, Duke of Lancaster</a>, and Blanche of Lancaster, and a grandson of King Edward III. He took the name Bolingbroke from his birthplace. As a youth he rode, hunted and jousted, and was besieged with Richard II in the Tower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peasants-revolt">Peasants' Revolt</a> of 1381.</p><p>He joined the Lords Appellant's rebellion against his cousin in 1387, though Richard later made him Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hereford">Hereford</a>. He went on crusading expeditions with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> in 1390 and 1392, and in 1392–93 made a pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1398 a remark attributed to Thomas de Mowbray, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norfolk">Norfolk</a>, led to a planned duel, which Richard halted by exiling Henry, and Mowbray for life. When Gaunt died in February 1399, Richard cancelled the documents allowing Henry to inherit automatically. Henry returned from exile with Thomas Arundel, the former archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a>, while Richard was campaigning in Ireland, and gathered enough support to have himself declared king, imprison Richard and bypass Richard's heir-presumptive, Edmund de Mortimer, Earl of March. Richard died in prison in February 1400, probably of starvation, whether self-imposed or forced being a matter of historical speculation.</p><p>He put down the Epiphany Rising in January 1400, and invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> in August 1400, a campaign that accomplished little. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-glyndwr">Owain Glyndŵr</a> declared himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a> in 1400. The Percy rebellions followed, and in 1403 the royal army defeated them at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shrewsbury">Shrewsbury</a>, where Henry Percy, known as Hotspur, was killed and the king's eldest son, Henry of Monmouth, was wounded by an arrow in the face. In 1401 the statute De heretico comburendo prescribed burning for heretics, to suppress the Lollards.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>From 1405 Henry suffered a disfiguring skin disease and acute attacks of a grave illness, whose nature medical historians still debate. His son Henry of Monmouth took over much of the government in 1410. Henry died on 20 March 1413 in the Jerusalem Chamber at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a>, during a meeting of Parliament.</p><p>He was buried not at Westminster but at Canterbury Cathedral beside the shrine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-becket">Thomas Becket</a>, with his second wife, Joan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>. Historians connect the choice with his wish to legitimise his dynasty. His son succeeded as Henry V, and the usurpation later contributed to the dynastic disputes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-roses">Wars of the Roses</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henry IV of England” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the early fourteenth century the main enemy was pagan Lithuania: the Order mounted almost annual raids, the Reisen, into Samogitia and Lithuania, joined by noble guests such as <b>Henry Bolingbroke</b>, the future Henry IV of England, who campaigned with the knights in 1390.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Richard II, overthrown by his cousin <b>Henry Bolingbroke</b> in 1399, died in captivity around this date, probably starved to death.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-14">14 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Richard II's abdication was read to an assembly at Westminster, which then acclaimed <b>Henry Bolingbroke</b> as Henry IV.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-30">30 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1405 <b>Henry IV</b> granted the island to Sir John Stanley.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-mann">Lordship of Mann</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bolingbroke Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Westminster, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.17,0.02&amp;year=1396" aria-label="Bolingbroke Castle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4550 334 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Henry IV of England"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5000 604h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5000 604h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.17,0.02&amp;year=1396">What was here in 1396? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
